  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 229 AM CDT Thu Oct 15 2026\n\nA cold front now well south of the area early this morning will lift \nnorthward as a surface low developing in the southern Plains lifts \nnortheastward toward central IL. The front should lift through \ncentral IL during the afternoon hours, bringing 70 degree dewpoints \nto much of the area and SBCAPE values of 1000-1500 J/kg to around to \nI-72 corridor, coincident with 40-50 kt deep layer shear. 150-250 \nStorm Relative Helicity values are also forecast in this area, \nsuggesting a few tornadoes will be possible. SPC has a Marginal Risk \nfor Severe thunderstorms for a threat of locally damaging wind gusts \nand a few tornadoes possible. The cold front trailing the low should \nbegin to push into west central IL by afternoon, and cross the \nremainder of central and southeast IL through the evening hours. \nHigh resolution models depict scattered showers developing mainly \nwest of I-57 this morning as an initial upper level disturbance and \nlow level moist-warm advection takes place. A small break is then \ndepicted before another disturbance and the warm front approaches \nmid morning into early afternoon. While many high-res models \ndecrease shower coverage during the afternoon hours, this is also \nthe period where a few models depict a few supercells developing in \nthe warm sector behind the warm front, with significant scatter \namong model solutions on when/where breaks or supercells will be \ndeveloping. More consistency develops with clearing behind the cold \nfront, as showers look to end west of I-55 by midnight, west of I-57 \nby 4 AM Friday, and throughout IL by 7 AM Friday. High resolution \nmodel ensembles depict average precipitation amounts of around a \nhalf inch, but also depict a few swaths of 1 to 3 inches. This \nrainfall will generally be beneficial with mostly dry conditions \npreceding the past few days, but 2 to 3 inch amounts could produce \nsome locally excessive amounts and WPC indicates a Marginal Risk for \nexcessive rainfall from around I-72 southward.\n\nBehind tonight's strong cold front, a strong pressure gradient \ndevelops over the area for Friday, which should set up 15-20 mph \nnorthwest winds, with gusts 30-40 mph, with the higher values west \nof I-55. A potent upper level shortwave and 500 mb cold pocket of \nminus 25 C will promote scattered shower activity and cloud cover. \nHighs should only reach the mid 50s for a very cold/blustery feel \ncompared with recent record warmth.\n\nFriday night, high pressure will migrate eastward toward the \nMississippi Valley, weakening the pressure gradient over especially \nwestern portions of central IL. With temperatures dropping into the \nmid 30s and the light winds, it appears areas of frost will develop \nwest of I-57, but to the east enough wind should remain to keep \ntemperatures in the upper 30s without frost. By Sunday morning, the \nhigh should migrate toward the mid-Atlantic coast, with winds \nalready beginning to pick up from the south by morning. \nNevertheless, mid-30s temperatures and frost potential looks to \ncover the entire area as the wind will likely be too little/too late \nto prevent it.\n\nSoutherly flow and a building ridge of high pressure will bring a \nwarming trend starting Sunday, raising highs from upper 50s Saturday \nto upper 60s Sunday. Further warming through midweek should bring \nhighs to mid to upper 70s by Wednesday. Dry conditions will continue \nSaturday through midweek under the influence of the surface high \nfollowed by upper ridge.",
